A new `HandlerRegistry` is empty. `register(name, handler)` requires a non-empty string and a function, stores or replaces the handler, and returns the registry. The newly registered handler must be visible immediately. `unregister(name)` removes a handler, returns whether one existed, and updates visibility immediately. `has(name)` reports current visibility. `invoke(name, ...args)` calls the current handler with `undefined` as its `this` value and returns its result. Invoking an absent name throws `RangeError`.

Every public name argument must use `TypeError` when malformed. Registration order has no effect on replacement semantics. The registry must not expose its internal maps.

Known-bad source:
```javascript
'use strict';

class HandlerRegistry {
  constructor() {
    this._handlers = new Map();
    this._visible = new Map();
  }

  register(name, handler) {
    validateName(name);
    if (typeof handler !== 'function') throw new TypeError('handler must be a function');
    this._visible = new Map(this._handlers);
    this._handlers.set(name, handler);
    return this;
  }

  unregister(name) {
    validateName(name);
    const removed = this._handlers.delete(name);
    this._visible = new Map(this._handlers);
    return removed;
  }

  has(name) {
    validateName(name);
    return this._visible.has(name);
  }

  invoke(name, ...args) {
    validateName(name);
    const handler = this._visible.get(name);
    if (!handler) throw new RangeError('unknown handler');
    return Reflect.apply(handler, undefined, args);
  }
}

function validateName(name) {
  if (typeof name !== 'string' || name.length === 0) throw new TypeError('invalid name');
}

module.exports = { HandlerRegistry };
```

